  "rationale": "Round 1 explored only iterative and unrolled_k, both of which produce one result every several cycles. The best throughput seen was 8.1 MSPS (iterative) and 6.7 MSPS (unrolled_k), far below the 32 MSPS constraint. These families are structurally incapable of meeting the throughput requirement regardless of parameter tuning. The pipelined and pipelined_m families produce one result per cycle, making throughput = Fmax, which can plausibly reach 32+ MSPS. The accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) was already met by several iterative designs (e.g., 2^-12.43, 2^-12.84), so accuracy is achievable. The sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 us constraint requires 32 results within 0.5 us of a tick; with 1 result/cycle at >= 32 MHz, 32 results take 1 us at 32 MHz, but the batch constraint is on the time from tick to last result, which for a pipelined unit with N+2 latency plus 31 cycles of throughput means the last result arrives at (N+2+31)/Fmax us. At Fmax >= 64 MHz and small N, this can fit in 0.5 us. The pipelined families are the only path to feasibility. Splitting 40 evals each between pipelined and pipelined_m allows NSGA-II to map the area/accuracy/throughput front for both, with pipelined_m likely offering lower area due to fewer registers."
